@subsection Game controllers and virtual pads
@cindex gamepad, remapping
@cindex joystick, remapping
@cindex virtual gamepad
@cindex uinput

Game controllers work in both directions.  As @emph{sources}: a rule
with @code{:match '(:id "045e:028e" :type gamepad)} (or
@code{joystick}) claims a gamepad, and its buttons (@code{BTN_SOUTH},
@code{pad-a}, @code{left-bumper}), stick directions (@code{ABS_X+},
@code{left-stick-up}, @code{dpad-left}) and whole axes (@code{ABS_RX},
@code{right-stick-x}) become inputs.  gowl reads the pad itself
(libinput ignores joysticks) and opens it only once a rule matches;
@code{:grab t} hides it from the game while it is remapped.  As
@emph{outputs}: @code{:virtual-pad "NAME"} declares a gamepad made
through @file{/dev/uinput} (named @samp{gowl virtual pad: NAME}),
created on its first press and destroyed when no rule declares it,
when the module is disabled and when the compositor stops.

@lisp
;; Left stick as WASD, hidden from the game
(cmacs-gowl-input-remap-define "stick-wasd"
  :match '(:id "045e:028e" :type gamepad)
  :grab t
  :analog '(:press 0.5 :release 0.3)
  :map '((left-stick-up . (key KEY_W)) (left-stick-left . (key KEY_A))
         (left-stick-down . (key KEY_S)) (left-stick-right . (key KEY_D))))

;; Right stick as the mouse (continuous: a declared exception)
(cmacs-gowl-input-remap-define "pad-mouse"
  :match '(:id "045e:028e" :type gamepad)
  :analog '(:pointer-speed 1500 :scroll-rate 12)
  :map '((right-stick-x . (pointer x)) (right-stick-y . (pointer y))
         (left-stick-y . (scroll vertical)) (pad-a . (button left))))

;; A keypad as a controller, for a game that only reads gamepads
(cmacs-gowl-input-remap-define "keypad-pad"
  :match '(:id "1234:5678" :type keyboard)
  :virtual-pad "keypad"
  :map '((KEY_KP8 . (stick left :y -1)) (KEY_KP2 . (stick left :y 1))
         (KEY_KP4 . (stick left :x -1)) (KEY_KP6 . (stick left :x 1))
         (KEY_KP0 . (pad-button BTN_SOUTH)) (KEY_KPENTER . (trigger right))))
@end lisp

@table @code
@item (pointer x) @r{/} (pointer y)
A whole axis moves the pointer while deflected (continuous).
@item (scroll vertical)
A whole axis scrolls in wheel notches while deflected (continuous).
@item (stick @var{side} :x @var{v})
Holds the virtual stick (@code{left}, @code{right}, @code{dpad}) while
the input is held.
@item (stick @var{side})
On @code{mouse-x} / @code{mouse-y}: the virtual stick follows the
mouse (continuous).
@item (trigger @var{side} [@var{value}])
Pulls a virtual trigger while held.
@item (pad-button @var{btn})
Holds a virtual button while held.
@end table

Held keys on one stick combine: opposite directions cancel and W+D is
up-right on the unit circle.  A stick direction used as a key is one
press through hysteresis (@code{:press} / @code{:release} in
@code{:analog}), so it stays inside one-to-one; the continuous targets
are listed in each rule's @code{continuous} field by
@code{cmacs-gowl-input-remap-list}.  @code{:analog} also takes
@code{:deadzone}, @code{:invert} (a list of axes), @code{:centred},
@code{:pointer-curve}, @code{:pointer-accel}, @code{:mouse-gain} and
@code{:mouse-idle-ms}.  @code{cmacs-gowl-input-remap-pads} lists the
virtual pads.

@strong{Permissions.}  Reading a controller needs read access to its
@file{/dev/input/event@var{N}} (the @code{uaccess} ACL a desktop gives
joystick nodes); a virtual pad needs write access to
@file{/dev/uinput}.  Without it the rule still loads, the first press
logs why, and @code{cmacs-gowl-input-remap-status} shows
@code{pad-error}.  The live session only ever opens a controller one
of your rules matches, so a test pad is invisible to it.  The schema,
the defaults and the worked examples are in
@file{deps/gowl/docs/input-remap.org}.
